# Folder — the loading adapter that reads declarations off a disk.
#
# The contract : it DECLARES the port it implements (the inverted arrow) ; the
# port never names it. `loaded_by("Folder")` resolves if and only if the loading
# port carries the `loaded_by` verb.
#
# It implements ONE convention, and naming it as an adapter is what makes it a
# convention rather than a law : a domain is a directory holding a `bluebook/`
# folder, its declarations are the files in there, and load order is DEPENDENCY
# order — ports declare the verb, an adapter declares a port, the hecksagon names
# both, the world supplies values. Alphabetical order would boot a hecksagon
# before the adapter it binds.
#
# A different loading adapter would be a different convention entirely : a gem
# that ships its declarations as constants, a manifest listing paths, a database
# of domains. Each is additive against this port ; none requires touching boot.
#
# It is a DRIVEN adapter — boot asks it for declarations and it answers. The
# filesystem does not reach in and start anything.
#
# It declares NO fields : which path to read is an argument to boot, not a
# per-deployment value.
Hecks.adapter "Folder" do
  port "loading"
end
